## Fraud and the desk

The card fraud model decides at the threshold that minimises what fraud costs us, not at the
one that maximises accuracy. On the three test months it would have cost
{{ v('models.fraud_card.cost_op') }} in declined good transactions and missed fraud, against
{{ v('models.fraud_card.cost_all') }} if we approved everything and
{{ v('models.fraud_card.cost_acc') }} at the threshold accuracy would pick. It declines with
precision {{ v('models.fraud_card.precision_op') }} and catches {{ v('models.fraud_card.recall_op') }}
of fraud. The analyst queue takes only alerts at the stricter queue point, where precision is
{{ v('models.fraud_card.precision_queue') }}.

I would ask the board to read our fraud figures the way the desk does. A fraud label arrives only
when the chargeback window closes, {{ v('policy.chargeback_window_days') }} days after the
transaction. At the end of August, {{ v('models.fraud_card.unlabelled_share') }} of the summer's
decisions were still unlabelled, and precision over the labelled ones was
{{ v('models.fraud_card.labelled_precision_as_of') }}. A report that counted the unlabelled
declines as mistakes would have shown {{ v('models.fraud_card.naive_precision_as_of') }}. We never
publish that number.

## Financial crime

Our four monitoring rules fired {{ v('f05.alerts') }} alerts over the three years, about
{{ v('f05.alerts_per_month') }} a month, and found {{ v('f05.episodes_found') }} of the
{{ v('f05.episodes') }} complete laundering episodes planted in our data to test them. Structuring
is where our investigators' time goes: legitimate cash businesses deposit just under the
reporting threshold, and only {{ v('f05.structuring_precision') }} of structuring alerts are
real. The queue is ordered by a triage model so the likeliest cases are opened first; it never
closes an alert on its own. No suspicious activity report is filed from this data.

